{"id": 3783438, "title": "CVE-2026-11352: QUIC zero-length UDP datagrams busy-loop", "submitted": "2026-06-05", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/3783438", "excerpt": "## Summary:\ncurl's QUIC UDP receive helper ignores zero-length UDP datagrams before counting them against the per-call packet budget. On Linux, `recvmmsg_packets()` loops while `pkts < max_pkts`, but `if(!mmsg[i].msg_len) continue;` runs before `pkts` is incremented. The `recvmsg_packets()` backend has the same no-progress pattern for `if(!nread) continue;`. A malicious HTTP/3/QUIC peer can continuously send zero-length UDP datagrams to the connected QUIC socket and keep curl inside `vquic_recv_packets(..., max_pkts=1000, ...)`. In my end-to-end CLI PoC with a real HTTP/3 server that completed the QUIC handshake before switching to zero-length datagrams, `curl --http3-only --max-time 2` was still running after 30 seconds and had to be killed; the same setup also reproduced with `WAIT_SECS=60` locally. The fallback `recvfrom_packets()` already counts a received datagram before skipping zero-length payloads, which indicates the safer intended budget accounting.\n\n## Affected version\nValidated on Linux x86_64 with an HTTP/3-enabled curl built from the signed `curl-8_20_0` release tag. In this repository snapshot the tag's `curlver.h` still reports `8.20.0-DEV`, but the tag object is `curl-8_20_0` with tag message `8.20.0`.\n\n`curl -V` from the reproduced build:\n\n```text\ncurl 8.20.0-DEV (Linux) libcurl/8.20.0-DEV OpenSSL/3.5.6 zlib/1.2.11 libpsl/0.21.0 ngtcp2/1.23.0 nghttp3/1.16.0\nRelease-Date: [unreleased]\nProtocols: dict file ftp ftps gopher gophers http https imap imaps ipfs ipns mqtt mqtts pop3 pop3s rtsp smtp smtps telnet tftp ws wss\nFeatures: alt-svc AsynchDNS HSTS HTTP3 HTTPS-proxy IPv6 Largefile libz PSL SSL threadsafe TLS-SRP UnixSockets\n```\n\nSource tags checked:\n\n- `curl-8_18_0`, `curl-8_19_0`, and `curl-8_20_0` contain the vulnerable zero-length skip before `pkts` is advanced in `recvmmsg_packets()` and `recvmsg_packets()`.\n- `curl-8_17_0` does not contain this same zero-length skip pattern.\n- Current master / `8.21.0-DEV` also contains the pattern.\n\nRelevant code in `lib/vquic/vquic.c`:\n\n```c\nwhile(pkts < max_pkts) {\n  ...\n  if(!mmsg[i].msg_len)\n    continue;\n  ...\n  pkts += (mmsg[i].msg_len + gso_size - 1) / gso_size;\n}\n\nfor(pkts = 0; pkts < max_pkts;) {\n  ...\n  if(!nread)\n    continue;\n  ...\n  pkts += (nread + gso_size - 1) / gso_size;\n}\n\n/* contrast: recvfrom_packets() */\n++pkts;\nif(!nread)\n  continue;\n```\n\n## Steps To Reproduce:\nRun the following s", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "likely_valid", "final_label": "Confirmed by the code", "tone": "good", "explain": "The real code shows the flaw the report describes.", "confidence": 0.92, "judge_verdict": "likely_valid", "receipts_override": "", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "grounded", "rev": "3d721a1d41a7", "rev_source": "master on 2026-06-05", "counts": {"grounded": 16, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 0}, "problems": []}, "summary": "curl's QUIC UDP receive helper ignores zero-length UDP datagrams before counting them against the per-call packet budget, causing a busy-loop that can keep curl running past its timeout.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "recvmsg_packets() and recvmmsg_packets() skip zero-length datagrams without incrementing the packet counter, leading to a busy-loop when a peer sends many zero-length UDP datagrams.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "Both helpers check for zero-length payloads before incrementing the packet counter pkts. The loop condition 'pkts < max_pkts' therefore makes no progress when only zero-length datagrams arrive.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/vquic/vquic.c", "line": 596, "note": "recvmsg_packets: 'if(!nread) continue;' at line 597 skips pkts increment at line 607", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/vquic/vquic.c", "line": 511, "note": "recvmmsg_packets: 'if(!mmsg[i].msg_len) continue;' at line 512 skips pkts increment at line 524", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "The bug is in lib/vquic/vquic.c where vquic_recv_packets() uses these helpers to receive packets.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "vquic_recv_packets dispatches to the two vulnerable helpers on Linux (HAVE_SENDMMSG) and other platforms with HAVE_SENDMSG.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/vquic/vquic.c", "line": 692, "note": "vquic_recv_packets calls recvmmsg_packets when HAVE_SENDMMSG is defined", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/vquic/vquic.c", "line": 694, "note": "vquic_recv_packets calls recvmsg_packets when HAVE_SENDMSG is defined", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "trigger", "text": "A malicious HTTP/3/QUIC peer sends continuous zero-length UDP datagrams after completing the QUIC handshake.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The attack vector is plausible: a QUIC peer that completed the handshake can send zero-length UDP datagrams to the connected socket, which will be processed by vquic_recv_packets via the vulnerable helpers.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/vquic/vquic.c", "line": 684, "note": "vquic_recv_packets is the entry point for receiving QUIC packets from the peer", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "impact", "text": "curl remains busy in vquic_recv_packets() and does not respect --max-time or other timeout settings, causing denial of service.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "Because pkts never increments on zero-length datagrams, the per-call budget max_pkts is never exhausted. The function does not return, so application-level timeouts (--max-time, CURLOPT_TIMEOUT) that are checked in the caller's event loop never get a chance to fire.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/vquic/vquic.c", "line": 684, "note": "vquic_recv_packets runs the busy loop; control does not return to the caller until the loop exits", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/altsvc.c", "line": 63, "note": "time() macro shows timeout handling is at a higher level that only runs when control returns", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "The provided script reproduces the issue by running a real HTTP/3 server, completing the QUIC handshake, then sending zero-length datagrams until curl is killed after 30 seconds.", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The reproduction script completes a real QUIC handshake, then floods zero-length datagrams via sendmmsg, and demonstrates curl --http3-only --max-time 2 running past 30 seconds.", "citations": [{"file": "script", "line": 0, "note": "The provided bash/Python script reproduces the issue end-to-end with a real HTTP/3 server and sendmmsg flood helpers", "valid": false}]}], "external_checks": [], "external_sources": [], "key_reasons": ["recvmsg_packets and recvmmsg_packets skip zero-length datagrams before incrementing pkts, defeating the per-call packet budget (lib/vquic/vquic.c lines 511-512, 596-597).", "The fallback recvfrom_packets increments pkts before the zero-length check (line 661 vs 665), confirming the intended safe accounting.", "vquic_recv_packets uses the vulnerable helpers on Linux (HAVE_SENDMMSG) and other platforms with HAVE_SENDMSG (lines 692, 694).", "A remote QUIC peer can send unlimited zero-length datagrams after handshake completion, keeping curl busy in vquic_recv_packets without returning to the event loop where --max-time is enforced.", "The project's own 'Busy-loops' policy states: 'If the problem bypasses the regular counter-measures and it causes a never-ending transfer, it might be a security problem.' This qualifies."], "missing_info": [], "reply": "Thank you for the detailed report and reproduction script. The code confirms that recvmsg_packets() and recvmmsg_packets() skip zero-length datagrams before incrementing the packet counter pkts (lib/vquic/vquic.c lines 511-512, 596-597), while the fallback recvfrom_packets() increments pkts first (line 661). This defeats the per-call max_pkts budget, allowing a remote QUIC peer to keep curl busy in vquic_recv_packets() indefinitely, bypassing --max-time. We will prepare a fix that moves the pkts increment before the zero-length check in both helpers.", "code": [{"name": "lib/vquic/vquic.c", "start": 538, "end": 616, "reason": "definition of recvmsg_packets", "rows": [{"n": 538, "text": "static CURLcode recvmsg_packets(struct Curl_cfilter *cf,", "notes": []}, {"n": 539, "text": "                                struct Curl_easy *data,", "notes": []}, {"n": 540, "text": "                                struct cf_quic_ctx *qctx,", "notes": []}, {"n": 541, "text": "                                size_t max_pkts,", "notes": []}, {"n": 542, "text": "                                vquic_recv_pkts_cb *recv_cb, void *userp)", "notes": []}, {"n": 543, "text": "{", "notes": []}, {"n": 544, "text": "  struct iovec msg_iov;", "notes": []}, {"n": 545, "text": "  struct msghdr msg;", "notes": []}, {"n": 546, "text": "  uint8_t buf[64 * 1024];", "notes": []}, {"n": 547, "text": "  struct sockaddr_storage remote_addr;", "notes": []}, {"n": 548, "text": "  size_t total_nread, pkts, calls;", "notes": []}, {"n": 549, "text": "  ssize_t rc;", "notes": []}, {"n": 550, "text": "  size_t nread;", "notes": []}, {"n": 551, "text": "  char errstr[STRERROR_LEN];", "notes": []}, {"n": 552, "text": "  CURLcode result = CURLE_OK;", "notes": []}, {"n": 553, "text": "  uint8_t msg_ctrl[CMSG_SPACE(sizeof(int))];", "notes": []}, {"n": 554, "text": "  size_t gso_size;", "notes": []}, {"n": 555, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 556, "text": "  DEBUGASSERT(max_pkts > 0);", "notes": []}, {"n": 557, "text": "  for(pkts = 0, total_nread = 0, calls = 0; pkts < max_pkts;) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 558, "text": "    /* fully initialize this on each call to `recvmsg()`. There seem to", "notes": []}, {"n": 559, "text": "     * operating systems out there that mess with `msg_iov.iov_len`. */", "notes": []}, {"n": 560, "text": "    memset(&msg, 0, sizeof(msg));", "notes": []}, {"n": 561, "text": "    msg_iov.iov_base = buf;", "notes": []}, {"n": 562, "text": "    msg_iov.iov_len = (int)sizeof(buf);", "notes": []}, {"n": 563, "text": "    msg.msg_iov = &msg_iov;", "notes": []}, {"n": 564, "text": "    msg.msg_iovlen = 1;", "notes": []}, {"n": 565, "text": "    msg.msg_control = msg_ctrl;", "notes": []}, {"n": 566, "text": "    msg.msg_name = &remote_addr;", "notes": []}, {"n": 567, "text": "    msg.msg_namelen = sizeof(remote_addr);", "notes": []}, {"n": 568, "text": "    msg.msg_controllen = sizeof(msg_ctrl);", "notes": []}, {"n": 569, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 570, "text": "    while((rc = recvmsg(qctx->sockfd, &msg, 0)) == -1 &&", "notes": []}, {"n": 571, "text": "          (SOCKERRNO == SOCKEINTR || SOCKERRNO == SOCKEMSGSIZE))", "notes": []}, {"n": 572, "text": "      ;", "notes": []}, {"n": 573, "text": "    if(!curlx_sztouz(rc, &nread)) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 574, "text": "      if(SOCKERRNO == EAGAIN || SOCKERRNO == SOCKEWOULDBLOCK) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 575, "text": "        goto out;", "notes": []}, {"n": 576, "text": "      }", "notes": []}, {"n": 577, "text": "      if(!cf->connected && SOCKERRNO == SOCKECONNREFUSED) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 578, "text": "        struct ip_quadruple ip;", "notes": []}, {"n": 579, "text": "        if(!Curl_cf_socket_peek(cf->next, data, NULL, NULL, &ip))", "notes": []}, {"n": 580, "text": "          failf(data, \"QUIC: connection to %s port %u refused\",", "notes": []}, {"n": 581, "text": "                ip.remote_ip, ip.remote_port);", "notes": []}, {"n": 582, "text": "        result = CURLE_COULDNT_CONNECT;", "notes": []}, {"n": 583, "text": "        goto out;", "notes": []}, {"n": 584, "text": "      }", "notes": []}, {"n": 585, "text": "      curlx_strerror(SOCKERRNO, errstr, sizeof(errstr));", "notes": []}, {"n": 586, "text": "      failf(data, \"QUIC: recvmsg() unexpectedly returned %zd (errno=%d; %s)\",", "notes": []}, {"n": 587, "text": "            rc, SOCKERRNO, errstr);", "notes": []}, {"n": 588, "text": "      result = CURLE_RECV_ERROR;", "notes": []}, {"n": 589, "text": "      goto out;", "notes": []}, {"n": 590, "text": "    }", "notes": []}, {"n": 591, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 592, "text": "    total_nread += nread;", "notes": []}, {"n": 593, "text": "    ++calls;", "notes": []}, {"n": 594, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 595, "text": "    /* A 0-length UDP packet is no QUIC packet */", "notes": []}, {"n": 596, "text": "    if(!nread)", "notes": ["C1: recvmsg_packets: 'if(!nread) continue;' at line 597 skips pkts increment at line 607"]}, {"n": 597, "text": "      continue;", "notes": []}, {"n": 598, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 599, "text": "    gso_size = vquic_msghdr_get_udp_gro(&msg);", "notes": []}, {"n": 600, "text": "    if(gso_size == 0)", "notes": []}, {"n": 601, "text": "      gso_size = nread;", "notes": []}, {"n": 602, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 603, "text": "    result = recv_cb(buf, nread, gso_size,", "notes": []}, {"n": 604, "text": "                     msg.msg_name, msg.msg_namelen, 0, userp);", "notes": []}, {"n": 605, "text": "    if(result)", "notes": []}, {"n": 606, "text": "      goto out;", "notes": []}, {"n": 607, "text": "    pkts += (nread + gso_size - 1) / gso_size;", "notes": []}, {"n": 608, "text": "  }", "notes": []}, {"n": 609, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 610, "text": "out:", "notes": []}, {"n": 611, "text": "  if(total_nread || result)", "notes": []}, {"n": 612, "text": "    CURL_TRC_CF(data, cf,", "notes": []}, {"n": 613, "text": "                \"vquic_recvmsg(len=%zu, packets=%zu, calls=%zu) -> %d\",", "notes": []}, {"n": 614, "text": "                total_nread, pkts, calls, result);", "notes": []}, {"n": 615, "text": "  return result;", "notes": []}, {"n": 616, "text": "}", "notes": []}], "cited": true}, {"name": "lib/vquic/vquic.c", "start": 434, "end": 535, "reason": "definition of rec